From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObliviOS Build <build@oblivios.local>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 17:55:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] memory: stop a background user when the device switches away
 from it

The Duress decoy user is meant to stay stopped between uses, but the
return path only switches back to user 0 and stock
UserController.stopUserOnSwitchIfEnforced is off, so on stallion the
decoy stayed running unlocked for 19 hours with 8 processes and about
0.6 GB of anon memory, and every broadcast restarted its processes.

Set the stock fw.stop_bg_users_on_switch property that UserController
already reads. The system user is never stopped by it.
---
 config/mk/google_devices/common/device-common.mk | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/config/mk/google_devices/common/device-common.mk b/config/mk/google_devices/common/device-common.mk
--- a/config/mk/google_devices/common/device-common.mk
+++ b/config/mk/google_devices/common/device-common.mk
@@ -68,6 +68,16 @@ PRODUCT_PACKAGES += restrict-pixel-health-association
 
 PRODUCT_PRODUCT_PROPERTIES += persist.vendor.intelligence=off
 
+# ObliviOS: stop a background user when the device switches away from it. The Duress decoy
+# user is meant to stay stopped between uses (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-21-duress-decoy-
+# user-switch-design.md), but the return path (ReturnToRealUserHelper) only switches back, and
+# stock UserController.stopUserOnSwitchIfEnforced is off unless this property or
+# config_multiuserDelayUserDataLocking says otherwise. Left running, the decoy keeps about 0.6 GB
+# of anon memory (8 processes, restarted by every broadcast) until the next reboot, measured on
+# stallion. This sets the stock property UserController already reads; the system user is never
+# stopped by it, and the guest user is already stopped on switch by stock code.
+PRODUCT_PRODUCT_PROPERTIES += fw.stop_bg_users_on_switch=1
+
 # ObliviOS: AirPods compatibility app (ADR-118). Kept in the
 # common file so every Pixel target ships it. The two XML modules are the privapp
 # allow-list and the single-package hidden-API entry; the app needs both.
